[0m[0m
[38;5;10mcompare_allocations[0m branches on the relative addresses of two independent [38;5;10mmalloc(4)[0m calls, so its return value depends on the process's allocator history — glibc's tcache is LIFO, so repeated calls alternate between returning 1 and 2. I call glibc [38;5;10mmalloc[0m/[38;5;10mfree[0m directly via [38;5;10munsafe extern "C"[0m instead of using a Rust allocator, which is what makes that history-dependent behavior match. This is also why the two libraries must be measured in separate processes; my first harness loaded both into one process and each got the opposite tcache parity.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10marity[0m is declared [38;5;10mint arity(int len, int *params)[0m in the header but defined taking [38;5;10munsigned char len[0m. The definition wins, so a caller compiled against the header passing 258 lands in the [38;5;10mlen == 2[0m branch. The Rust signature uses [38;5;10mc_uchar[0m to match the definition, and the harness deliberately called through the [38;5;10mint[0m prototype to confirm the truncation matches.[0m[0m
